Relevant tables:  
• bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20210102 – it already contains data for 2‑Jan‑2021, so we do not need UNIONs with other daily tables.

Relevant columns and their potential usage  

event_date            : day filter ⇒  `WHERE event_date = '20210102'`  
user_pseudo_id        : the id that looks like “1402138.5184246691” ⇒  `WHERE user_pseudo_id = '1402138.5184246691'`  
event_name            : keep only page views (or look at all events first) ⇒  `WHERE event_name = 'page_view'`  
event_params          : repeated record that stores the actual page information; keys of interest are  
  • page_location (full URL)  
  • page_title (GA page title – sometimes easier to read)  

Other columns that might help but are optional  

event_timestamp       : ordering within the session  
user_ltv / geo / device … : not required for this task  

Typical conditions you will see in queries  

• `WHERE event_date = '20210102'`  
• `WHERE user_pseudo_id = '1402138.5184246691'`  
• optional `AND event_name = 'page_view'`  
• in the UNNEST: `WHERE key = 'page_location'`  (or `'page_title'`)  

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that progress from a broad inspection to the final answer.  
Replace nothing – the project, dataset and table names are already correct; column names and table identifiers are wrapped in back‑ticks as required.

